      <!-- Detailed descriptions for each accessory type -->
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-12 mb-4">Accessory-by-Accessory Buying Guide</h2>

      <div class="space-y-10 mt-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Silk Fans (团扇 tuánshàn)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">The round silk fan is the most iconic Chinese accessory — you've seen it in every Tang dynasty drama. Hand-painted fans are created by Suzhou artisans who paint landscapes, flowers, or calligraphy directly onto stretched silk. The frame is traditionally sandalwood or bamboo, and a genuine hand-painted fan takes 2-3 days to produce. <strong>How to spot quality:</strong> hold the fan up to light — hand-painted silk shows subtle brush-stroke variations; machine-printed designs look uniformly flat. The handle should feel slightly heavier than expected (indicating real wood, not plastic). Vendors on Pingjiang Road in Suzhou will paint custom designs while you wait — bring a photo or Chinese character you'd like painted. A quality hand-painted fan should last decades if kept dry.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Jade Pendants (玉佩 yùpèi)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">Jade (玉 yù) has been China's most treasured stone for over 7,000 years, symbolizing purity, moral integrity, and protection. The classic pendant is a flat disc (璧 bì) or a lock-shaped piece (长命锁 chángmìngsuǒ) worn on a red silk cord around the neck — the latter is a traditional gift for children and travelers, believed to "lock in" long life. <strong>What you'll actually find in markets:</strong> genuine nephrite jade from Hetian (Xinjiang) is pale green to white and costs 500-5,000+ RMB. Most tourist-market "jade" is actually jadeite, serpentine, or dyed quartz. If you want a genuine piece, shop at a certified jewelry store (look for the "中国宝玉石" certification sign), not a street stall. For a beautiful souvenir without the investment risk, jadeite pendants at 50-100 RMB still have that cool, smooth weight that makes jade special. The Hongqiao Pearl Market in Beijing has the widest selection.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Hairpins (发簪 fàzān)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">In ancient China, a hairpin was more than decoration — receiving your first hairpin at age 15 marked the transition to adulthood in the ji-li (笄礼) coming-of-age ceremony. Today's hairpins range from simple single-prong metal pins (簪) to elaborate two-prong combs (钗 chāi) with dangling ornaments, flowers, or phoenix motifs. <strong>Material guide:</strong> brass and copper pins (30-50 RMB) are everyday-wear quality; silver pins (80-150 RMB) resist tarnish; cloisonné (景泰蓝 jǐngtàilán) pins with colored enamel inlay are the most distinctly Chinese style. Xi'an's Shuyuanmen Street has the best concentration of hairpin shops near the Hanfu rental district — you can try pins with your rental costume to see what works before buying. Wood pins (桃木 táomù, peach wood) are the lightest and most comfortable for all-day wear.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Chinese Knots (中国结 zhōngguójié)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">The Chinese knot is a single cord woven into a symmetrical pattern without beginning or end — a metaphor for eternity and continuity. Each knot style carries meaning: the double coin knot (双钱结) for wealth, the button knot (纽扣结) for unity, the pan chang knot (盘长结) for the Buddhist endless knot representing life's interconnectedness. These make excellent small gifts: they're lightweight, indestructible in luggage, and available at literally every tourist market in China. The most common form is a red knot with a tassel, often hung in homes for luck. <strong>Quality check:</strong> good knots are woven from a single silk cord (no glue joints), feel dense in the hand, and have evenly spaced loops. The best knots come from Beijing's Panjiayuan Market on weekends — look for elderly artisans who weave knots on-site.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Embroidered Shoes (绣花鞋 xiùhuāxié)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">Traditional Chinese embroidered shoes feature silk upper fabric with hand-stitched floral designs and the distinctive "cloud sole" (云头 yúntóu) — a thick, layered fabric sole with an upward-curling toe. In the Ming and Qing dynasties, embroidery patterns on shoes signaled social status: peonies for wealth, lotus for purity, butterflies for marital happiness. <strong>For travelers:</strong> ready-made embroidered flats (80-200 RMB) are sold in most tourist markets and work beautifully as indoor slippers or light walking shoes. Custom pairs (300-600 RMB) from Suzhou cobblers take 3-5 days and can be fitted with modern rubber soles for actual outdoor use. <strong>Sizing note:</strong> Chinese shoe sizing runs differently — always try on. If buying as a gift, trace the recipient's foot on paper and bring the tracing.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Oil-Paper Umbrellas (油纸伞 yóuzhǐsǎn)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">Hangzhou's Yuhang district has produced oil-paper umbrellas for over 400 years — the same craft that supplies props for Chinese period films. These umbrellas are made from bamboo frames covered with mulberry-bark paper, then coated in tong oil (桐油 tóngyóu) for water resistance. The result is surprisingly durable: a well-made umbrella can withstand light rain for years. Each umbrella requires 70+ manual steps to produce. <strong>Practical for travel:</strong> smaller folding umbrellas (50-80 RMB) fit in suitcases; larger ceremonial umbrellas (150-400 RMB) are stunning photo props but unwieldy to transport. The best shop is the Yuhang Oil-Paper Umbrella Workshop, a 40-minute drive from Hangzhou's West Lake. In the city, Hangzhou Silk City sells a good selection.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Miao Silver (苗族银饰 miáozú yínshì)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">The Miao (Hmong) people of Guizhou are legendary silversmiths who believe silver wards off evil spirits and embodies the light of the moon. Miao silverwork is immediately recognizable: heavy, ornate pieces with intricate patterns of butterflies (the mother ancestor), dragons, fish, and geometric borders — all hand-hammered using techniques passed through maternal lines. A full Miao festival headdress can weigh 5-10 kilograms and represents an entire family's wealth; these are museum pieces, not souvenirs. <strong>What to buy:</strong> bracelets (100-300 RMB) and necklaces (200-500 RMB) are wearable daily. Earrings (50-150 RMB) are lightweight and easy to pack. Shop in Kaili's Sunday textile market or in the villages of Xijiang and Langde. <strong>Authenticity check:</strong> real Miao silver is slightly soft (you can make a shallow scratch with a fingernail), has a warm white luster (not chrome-bright), and the back shows hammer marks, not machining.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-warm-800">Silk Scarves (丝巾 sījīn)</h3>
          <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed mt-2">Hangzhou silk has been China's gold standard since the Tang dynasty — the city's mulberry groves and skilled weavers supplied the imperial court and the Silk Road trade. Today, a Hangzhou silk scarf is the most practical traditional accessory: wearable at home, packs flat, and costs far less than a full garment. <strong>Types:</strong> printed scarves (50-150 RMB) feature Chinese landscape paintings or floral patterns transferred onto silk twill. Hand-rolled scarves (200-500 RMB) have edges finished by artisans using a technique called 卷边 (juǎnbiān) — tiny, tight silk rolls that take a skilled worker 30-45 minutes per scarf. <strong>Quality test:</strong> rub the silk between your fingers — it should feel smooth and cool, not slippery (polyester) or rough (low-grade silk). Real silk also dissolves in bleach (a single thread test); polyester doesn't. Buy at Hangzhou Silk City (杭州丝绸城) — a multi-story market with 600+ vendors where you can compare quality side by side.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Suzhou: Fans & Silk Accessories</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed">Suzhou''s Pingjiang Road and Shantang Street are lined with shops selling hand-painted silk fans (团扇), embroidered pouches, and hair ornaments. The Suzhou Silk Museum offers context before you shop, and the prices are more reasonable than Shanghai or Beijing.</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hangzhou: Silk Scarves & Umbrellas</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed">Hangzhou Silk City is a multi-story market with hundreds of vendors. Oil-paper umbrellas (油纸伞) from nearby Yuhang are a specialty — lightweight, hand-painted, and surprisingly durable.</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Beijing: Jade & Jewelry</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed">Beijing''s Hongqiao Pearl Market and Panjiayuan Antique Market are treasure troves for jade pendants, embroidered shoes, and vintage accessories. Bargain hard — starting at 30-40% of the asking price is standard.</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Xi’an: Hanfu Hair Accessories & Ornaments</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed">Xi’an’s Hanfu rental streets near the South Gate are lined with accessory shops selling traditional hairpins, floral hairpieces, and tassels. The Muslim Quarter area has stores specializing in bronze mirrors and Tang dynasty-style ornaments. Budget 30–80 RMB for a quality hairpin set or 50–150 RMB for a complete hair accessory ensemble.</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Guizhou: Miao Silver & Embroidery Textiles</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed">For truly handcrafted pieces, head to the Miao villages near Kaili in Guizhou. Miao silversmiths create elaborate headdresses, neck rings, and bracelets using techniques passed down for generations. Textile markets in Kaili sell hand-embroidered bags, belts, and fabric pieces direct from artisans. These make exceptional souvenirs with a story behind them. Expect to pay 100–500 RMB for quality silver pieces and 50–200 RMB for embroidered textiles, depending on complexity.</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Look For When Buying</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-600 leading-relaxed">When buying traditional accessories, check for authentic regional materials: Suzhou silk, Beijing jade, Guizhou silver, Hangzhou brocade. Mass-produced souvenirs flood popular tourist streets, so walk a block or two from the main drag for better quality at lower prices. For fans and umbrellas, verify the frame is bamboo (not plastic) and the painting is hand-applied rather than printed. A good hand-painted silk fan costs 80–200 RMB; a machine-printed one, 20–40 RMB. Most shops will wrap purchases carefully for travel, but bring a small padded pouch for delicate jade or silver items.
    </div>
